        // That works regardless of whether the call reverted or not; in reference, revert leaves credit >0; in mutant, no revert and credit becomes 0.
        // So we don't need try/catch; we just call withdrawAll (which may revert) inside a low-level call to bypass the revert.
        // We use vm.prank(failingReceiver) and a low-level call to make sure the test doesn't revert if the call reverts.

        // Corrected approach:
        // Deposit from failingReceiver (already done).
        // Call withdrawAll via low-level call to capture success boolean.
        (bool success, ) = address(c).call(abi.encodeWithSignature("withdrawAll()"));
        // But we need to prank as failingReceiver. We can use vm.prank with low-level call? vm.prank only affects the next call.
        // We can do: vm.prank(failingReceiver); (bool success, ) = address(c).call(...);
        // Then assert that credit of failingReceiver > 0 (reference) because either it reverted and credit >0, or it didn't and mutant cleared credit.
        // In reference, the call fails (reverts) because of require(callResult), so success=false and state unchanged -> credit >0.
        // In mutant, require is missing, call succeeds but transfer fails (Ether not sent, but state updated?), wait: In mutant, balance -= oCredit and credit=0 happen BEFORE the call.
        // Actually, in mutant, the order is: balance -= oCredit; credit[msg.sender] = 0; then call. So even if call fails, credit is already 0.
        // So after withdrawAll in mutant, credit=0 regardless of call success. In reference, if call fails, the require reverts, so credit remains >0.
        // Therefore, assertTrue(credit_after > 0) will succeed for reference and fail for mutant.
        // We need to ensure that the call to withdrawAll in reference does revert, and we capture that revert without halting the test.
        // The low-level call does that.

        // We'll redo setup to avoid earlier try/catch complexity.
        // The test already deployed Reverter, deposited from failingReceiver.
        // Now perform the low-level call.

        vm.prank(failingReceiver);
        (bool successLow, ) = address(c).call(abi.encodeWithSignature("withdrawAll()"));

        // In reference: successLow is false because require(callResult) reverts.
        // In mutant: successLow is true (no require, so call doesn't revert; the internal call fails but that doesn't propagate since return value unchecked).
        // But we only need to assert that credit of failingReceiver remains > 0.
        // This works regardless of successLow; we just read credit.
        assertTrue(c.credit(failingReceiver) > 0);
    }
}

